## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
<h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # What's Your Favorite Food? – Lesson # 5</h1>
<p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> It's Time to Eat.</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> What's Your Favorite Food?</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From <span style="text-decoration: underline;">___</span> to <span style="text-decoration: underline;">___</span></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Simplify written instructions for peers. Concept: Relate concepts to everyday scenarios.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify food-related instructions for peers by pointing and using simple words (e.g., pointing to an apple and saying "Take an apple."). Can relate food vocabulary to everyday scenarios with gestures (e.g., points to lunch and says, "I eat chicken at lunch."). Can support understanding of food-related words by rephrasing instructions (e.g., says, "pick food" while holding an apple").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Activities to activate prior knowledge, introduce the theme, and model target language for the lesson.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts a quick "Food Flashcard Review" game. Teacher shows flashcards of food items (apple, banana, sandwich, water, juice, rice with chicken, soup) one by one. Students call out the name of the food. Teacher then asks "What's your favorite food?" and elicits short answers or gestures.</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a flashcard of an apple. Students say "Apple!"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks, "Do you like apples?" Students respond, "Yes, I do!" or "No, I don't!"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models expressing likes and dislikes. "My <b>f</b>a<b>v</b>orite food is <b>s</b>andwich. I like <b>s</b>andwiches, but I don't like <b>s</b>oup." (Teacher emphasizes /f/ and /s/ sounds). The teacher then picks up an apple flashcard and says, "This is an apple. I can eat an apple." (Phonemic awareness: /ˈæ-p.əl/)</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a banana: "This is a banana. I like bananas. What's your favorite food, [Student A]?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'water' food or drink?" (Expected: "Drink!")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I like apples, but I don't like soup', do I like soup?" (Expected: "No!")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Introduction of new language or content, connecting to the scenario. Focus on comprehensible input.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher displays a simple, illustrated bar chart on the board or projector, showing the results of a "Favorite Food Survey" (e.g., Apple: 5 students, Banana: 3 students, Sandwich: 2 students). This chart represents the outcome of the 21st Century Skills Project students are working on in the unit. The teacher also has large print cards with simplified instructions.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Mediation: Text: Simplify written instructions for peers.</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to the chart and says: "Look! This is our Favorite Food Survey! Many people like apples!"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher then introduces the task: "Today, we will help our friends understand this chart with easy words."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a card with simplified instructions like:
            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Point to food.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Say "___ people like ___."</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher models explaining a part of the chart using gestures and simple words: points to "Apple" bar and says, "Look! Five people like apples." (Phonemic awareness: /ˈæ-p.əl/)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher then points to "Water" on another small chart if available and says, "Many people like to drink water." (Phonemic awareness: /ˈw-ɔːt-ər/)</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "How many people like bananas?" (Students point to banana bar and say "Three!")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Do more people like apples or sandwiches?" (Students point to the higher bar and say "Apples!")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Activities focusing on accuracy and controlled practice of the target language and mediation skill.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Simplifying food-related instructions/information; using present simple, WH- questions, "I like... but I don't..." and core food vocabulary (apple, banana, sandwich, lunch, breakfast, dinner, food, snack, water, juice, rice with chicken, soup; eat, drink, taste, choose, bring, want, enjoy, share, prepare, serve; can, can't; tasty, healthy, hungry, favorite, sweet).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Match the Simplified Explanation" (5 min)</b>
            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher distributes worksheets with pictures of small survey charts (e.g., "6 people like juice," "4 people like soup").</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Next to each chart, there are two simplified sentences. Students choose the correct sentence that describes the chart.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: Chart shows "Juice: 6." Options: a) "Six people like juice." b) "Four people like juice." Students circle 'a'.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Rephrase for Your Friend" (5 min)</b>
            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says a longer sentence, and students, in pairs, rephrase it more simply for their partner using gestures and pointing.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: Teacher says, "It is important for us to drink enough water every day to stay healthy."</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students rephrase: (points to water) "Drink water! Good for you!" or "Drink water. Stay healthy."</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Another example: Teacher says, "I really enjoy eating tasty bananas, but I cannot eat any sweet desserts."</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students rephrase: "I like bananas! But I don't like sweets." (Teacher reinforces "but" usage).</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Explain My Mini-Chart" (7 min)</b>
            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each pair receives a small, different, simple bar chart of favorite foods (e.g., "Rice with chicken: 4, Soup: 3" or "Breakfast: 5, Lunch: 3").</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A explains their chart to Student B using only simple words and pointing, like "Four people like rice with chicken." Student B listens and tries to draw or write the numbers for each food.</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then they switch roles. Teacher circulates to provide support and check for correct pronunciation of /f/ in favorite and /s/ in snack if used.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">17 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students apply the language in a realistic scenario, focusing on fluency and task completion.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative group explanation and presentation of the "Favorite Food Survey" results.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group has their completed "Favorite Food Survey" chart from the 21st Century Skills Project (or a pre-prepared one for this lesson if the project is still ongoing).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains: "Imagine a new friend comes to our class. They don't know our favorite foods! Your job is to tell them about our survey results using easy words and pointing."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Within each group, students take turns being the "explainer" and the "new friend." The explainer simplifies the survey chart information for the "new friend."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students must use simple sentences (e.g., "Three people like bananas."), pointing, and gestures to make the information clear. They should also practice using "I like... but I don't..." if they encounter foods with both positive and negative responses, or to contrast their own preferences with the survey results.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "new friend" can ask simple yes/no or WH-questions like "Do people like soup?" or "What's this food?" to prompt further simplification.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, observing groups, providing gentle correction for clarity and target language use, and encouraging sharing preferences respectfully. (e.g., "I like sancocho, but I am not a fan of broccoli.")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of students' achievement of the learning outcome and opportunities for self-reflection.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the group explanations, focusing on student ability to mediate information.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will explain their group's "Favorite Food Survey" chart to a peer (from another group), simplifying the information using basic words, pointing, and gestures. Each student takes a turn explaining a part of the chart.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of simplified explanation:</b> Student uses simple words and gestures to explain the survey results.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Employs words like "apple," "sandwich," "like," "don't like," "many," "few."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pointing and gestures:</b> Effectively uses non-verbal cues to support understanding.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Basic sentence structure:</b> Uses "X people like Y food" and simple "I like...but I don't..." sentences.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative interaction:</b> Engages respectfully with peers, showing politeness when sharing preferences.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Pronounces target words like "favorite" /f/ and "snack" /s/ clearly.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper or verbally:</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I use easy words to explain the food chart?" (Yes / No / A little)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did my friend understand my explanation?" (Yes / No / A little)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Opportunities for students and teacher to reflect on the lesson's effectiveness and plan for future learning.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of explaining the food chart was easy for you?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was hard, and why?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? (e.g., Students effectively used pointing and simple sentences to explain their survey results.)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students to better simplify information for peers? (e.g., Provide more visual sentence frames for explaining numbers, or explicitly model rephrasing more complex ideas into simpler ones.)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully integrate the use of "but" in compound sentences to express differing preferences? If not, how can this be reinforced?</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "What's Your Favorite Food?" theme. Students have now practiced all four skills and mediation, culminating in the presentation of their favorite food survey. The next theme will build on basic descriptive language and simple interactions.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>